# Known violations of the CSS architecture contract (web/css/README.md).
#
# Format: <check> <item>   # optional note
#
# This file is the outstanding debt of the CSS restructure. Entries may be
# removed, never added: a new violation is a build failure, and an entry that
# stops matching anything is also a failure, so the file cannot rot.
#
# The ownership block arrived in one go when the sheets were split and every
# `"owns": ["*"]` became a real list of roots. Until then the check could not
# see anything — one file owning everything cannot misfile a rule. Nothing got
# worse that day; the debt simply became countable. It only shrinks from here.
#
# Audit what is still owed:  node scripts/lint-css-arch --emit-allowlist

# --- ownership ------------------------------------------------------------
# A selector filed in a sheet manifest.json gives no claim on it: another sheet
# owns its root, and this one does not own its subject either. Each line is one
# root in one sheet. What is left was measured against its owners and stays
# where it is for the reason given beside it.

# The detached board (?view=pinboard): one mode, stated in the sheet of the
# feature that defines it, hiding shell pieces other sheets own. The header
# hides have to stay in components — .btn-icon-header (patterns) sets display
# on those buttons, and a layout-layer hide would lose to it once the layers
# are wrapped.
ownership components/pinboard.css:conversation-bar
ownership components/pinboard.css:conversation-tabs-container
ownership components/pinboard.css:sidebar-backdrop
ownership components/pinboard.css:header-actions
ownership components/pinboard.css:header-actions-controls


# --- layer-inversion -------------------------------------------------------
# A rule filed below a rule it overrides: the lower-layer rule wins today by
# being more specific, and would lose the moment the sheets are wrapped in
# their layers. One entry per file pair, `<lower> > <higher> <count>`, because
# the fix is per file pair and the findings run to thousands.
#
# The count is a ratchet: exceeding it fails, and so does beating it — the
# number comes down with the work. To see what is behind one:
#
#   node scripts/lint-css-arch --inversions <lower> <higher>
#
